  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]?$",
  "worked_solution": "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}13-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 12 & 13-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (13-\\lambda )(13-\\lambda )-144 & =0 \\\\ 169-26\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-144 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-26\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
